<!-- What exactly is React?
React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces, especially interfaces made from reusable components.

Manually managing all those DOM changes becomes difficult.
React provides a structured way to describe:
"This is what my UI should look like for the current state."
Instead of manually telling the browser every DOM operation.

JSX(JavaScript XML) allows us to write UI-like syntax inside JavaScript.

What is Vite?
Vite is a build tool and development server used to create and run modern frontend applications.
Vite gives you the development environment needed to:
run your React application
transform JSX
bundle your application for production
provide fast development updates
optimize your application for production.

node_modules
This contains installed dependencies.Also, it usually isn't committed to GitHub.
That's why .gitignore contains:node_modules

You can put JavaScript expressions inside {}.
{expression} works. But arbitrary JavaScript statements don't directly work there.
{if (age > 18) {
    ...
}}    -----not valid
instead, {age > 18 ? "Adult" : "Minor"}

Props = properties passed from a parent component to a child component.
Props are just an object. Props are read-only : A component should not modify its own props.

JavaScript object destructuring -
Instead of:

function Product(props) {
    return (
        <div>
            <h2>{props.name}</h2>
            <p>{props.price}</p>
        </div>
    );
}

we commonly write:

function Product({ name, price }) {
    return (
        <div>
            <h2>{name}</h2>
            <p>{price}</p>
        </div>
    );
}

Component Composition:
<Card>
    <h2>Hello</h2>
    <p>Welcome</p>
</Card>

function Card({ children }) {
    return (
        <div className="card">
            {children}
        </div>
    );
}

props vs children
The content between the opening and closing component tags becomes: children
Props
    ↓
Data passed Parent → Child

children
    ↓
Content passed through component tags

composition vs props:
<Card title="Product">
    <p>iPhone</p>
    <button>Buy</button>
</Card>

function Card({ title, children }) {
    return (
        <div className="card">
            <h2>{title}</h2>
            <div>
                {children}
            </div>
        </div>
    );
}

Dynamic rendering means:
The UI is generated based on data instead of manually writing every UI element.
function App() {
    return (
        <div>
            {products.map((product) => (
                <Product
                    name={product.name}
                    price={product.price}
                />
            ))}
        </div>
    );
}

React will give you a warning:
Each child in a list should have a unique "key" prop.
{products.map((product) => (
    <Product
        key={product.id}
        name={product.name}
        price={product.price}
    />
))}
avoid key={index}

Writing every prop manually becomes annoying.
You can use the spread operator:
<Product
    key={product.id}
    {...product}
/>

Conditional Rendering:
function App() {
    const isLoggedIn = true;

    return (
        <h1>
            {isLoggedIn ? "Welcome" : "Please Login"}
        </h1>
    );
}

function App() {
    const hasNotification = true;

    return (
        <div>
            <h1>Home</h1>

            {hasNotification && (
                <p>You have a new notification!</p>
            )}
        </div>
    );
}

You can't simply do:
<button onClick={greet("Arya")}>
because that executes immediately.
Instead:
<button onClick={() => greet("Arya")}>
    Greet
</button>

React gives your event handler an event object.
function handleClick(event) {
    console.log(event);
}
<button onClick={handleClick}>
    Click
</button>

State is data that React remembers for a component and that can trigger a re-render when updated.
Each component instance gets its own state.

setCount(prevCount => prevCount + 1);
This is called the functional updater form.

function handleClick() {
    setCount(count + 1);
    setCount(count + 1);
    setCount(count + 1);
}
All three calls can be based on the same current count value from that render.
Using:
setCount(prev => prev + 1);
setCount(prev => prev + 1);
setCount(prev => prev + 1);

const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
You should not do: items.push(newItem);
because you're mutating the existing array.
Instead:
setItems(prevItems => [
    ...prevItems,
    newItem
]);
We're creating a new array.

State Colocation -> Keep state as close as possible to the components that actually need it.Keep state at the lowest common ancestor of all components that need that state.

React applications commonly treat state as immutable.

That means:
Don't directly modify the existing state object/array. Create a new value instead.

Why?
Because React relies heavily on identity/reference changes to reason about whether values have changed.

Remove an item
const [items, setItems] = useState([
    { id: 1, name: "Apple" },
    { id: 2, name: "Banana" },
    { id: 3, name: "Mango" }
]);

Remove Banana:
setItems(prev =>
    prev.filter(item => item.id !== 2)
);



import { useState } from "react";

function App() {

    const [name, setName] = useState("");

    function handleSubmit(e) {
        e.preventDefault();

        console.log(name);
    }

    return (
        <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>

            <input
                type="text"
                value={name}
                onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)}
            />

            <button type="submit">
                Submit
            </button>

        </form>
    );
}




import { useState } from "react";

function App() {

    const [form, setForm] = useState({
        name: "",
        email: "",
        password: ""
    });

    function handleChange(e) {
        const { name, value } = e.target;

        setForm(prev => ({
            ...prev,
            [name]: value
        }));
    }

    function handleSubmit(e) {
        e.preventDefault();

        console.log(form);
    }

    return (
        <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>

            <input
                type="text"
                name="name"
                value={form.name}
                onChange={handleChange}
            />

            <input
                type="email"
                name="email"
                value={form.email}
                onChange={handleChange}
            />

            <input
                type="password"
                name="password"
                value={form.password}
                onChange={handleChange}
            />

            <button type="submit">
                Register
            </button>

        </form>
    );
}


With no dependency array:
useEffect(() => {
    console.log("Effect");
});
it runs after every render.

useEffect(() => {
    console.log("Effect");
}, []);
Now the effect runs after the component's initial mount.

[] is dependency array
